WN.lb(34,{"mt_qixeaiswFP":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which animal digs a burrow under a field?","choices":["A rabbit","A bird","A fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which animal has strong paws for digging?","explain":"A rabbit digs a burrow under the ground. It hides there, safe from foxes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can plants change a place?","choices":["Yes, roots can crack a path","No, plants are too soft","No, only animals can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the tree next to the path.","explain":"Plants change places too. Growing roots push so hard they can lift and crack paths and even split rocks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why do people dig a well?","choices":["To get water to drink","To make a home","To grow food"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is deep under the ground?","explain":"A well reaches water under the ground. People change the place to meet their need for water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You see little curly piles of soil on the grass. Who made them?","choices":["Worms","A beaver","A bird"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who lives in tunnels under the grass?","explain":"Worms push soil up out of their tunnels. The curly piles are called worm casts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why does a squirrel bury nuts?","choices":["To eat them later, in winter","To be naughty","To make a nest"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the squirrel do with the nuts when food is hard to find?","explain":"The squirrel buries nuts so it has food in winter. That change meets its need for food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A tree by a stream has been chewed to a point. Who did it?","choices":["A beaver","A rabbit","The wind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the clue: chewing teeth, near water.","explain":"A beaver chews trees with its big front teeth and uses the wood for its dam."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A bird puts {a} twigs in its nest in the morning and {b} more after lunch. How many twigs is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Put the two lots of twigs together.","explain":"{a} and {b} more make {=a+b} twigs. Twig by twig the bird changes the tree into a safe home for its eggs.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which change meets the need for food?","choices":["People plant a vegetable patch","A bird builds a nest","People dig a well"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one gives something to eat?","explain":"A vegetable patch grows food. A nest is a safe home and a well gives water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"{a} worms each push up 2 curly casts on the grass. How many casts are there?","answer":"a*2","hint":"Count 2 casts for each worm.","explain":"{a} worms with 2 casts each make {=a*2} casts. The casts are a clue that worms are tunnelling under the grass.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why does a beaver want a deep pond?","choices":["Its home is safe in deep water","It likes to splash","To dry up the stream"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who might try to catch a beaver?","explain":"The beaver's home sits in the pond with its door under water, so foxes and other hunters cannot get in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A beaver adds {a} sticks to its dam each night. How many sticks does it add in 3 nights?","answer":"a*3","hint":"Add {a} three times: one lot for each night.","explain":"{a} + {a} + {a} = {=a*3} sticks. Night after night the dam grows, and the pond behind it gets deeper.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A squirrel buries {a} nuts. In winter it digs up {b} of them to eat. How many nuts are still in the ground?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start at {a} and take away the {b} it ate.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b} nuts in the ground. Some of those forgotten nuts can grow into new trees, so the squirrel changes the wood too.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why does a bird build a nest?","choices":["To keep its eggs and chicks safe","To get water","For no reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what is inside a nest.","explain":"A nest is a safe home for eggs and chicks. The bird changes a tree or a hedge to meet that need."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A path next to a tree is lifted up and cracked. What did it?","choices":["The tree's roots","A rabbit","A worm"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is under the path, next to the tree?","explain":"Tree roots grow thicker every year and push hard from below. They can lift and crack a path."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What happens behind a beaver's dam?","choices":["The water backs up into a deep pond","The stream dries up","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"The dam is a wall across the stream. Where does the water go?","explain":"The dam holds the water back, so it fills up into a deep pond. The beaver's home sits safely in that pond."}],"explain":{"kid":["Living things change the places where they live. They do it to get what they need: food, water or a safe home.","A beaver chews down trees with its big front teeth. It piles sticks and mud across a stream to make a dam. The water backs up behind the dam and makes a deep pond. The beaver builds its home in the pond, where foxes cannot reach it.","Other animals change places too. A rabbit digs a burrow under a field. A bird builds a nest in a tree. Worms make tunnels in the soil. A squirrel buries nuts to eat in winter.","Plants change places too. Tree roots grow a bit thicker every year. They push so hard they can lift and crack a path. People change places as well. They dig wells for water and plant fields for food."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. This is NGSS kindergarten standard K-ESS2-2: children make a claim, backed by evidence, about how plants and animals, including people, change their environment to meet their needs. In England it links to Year 1 and 2 work on habitats and on what animals and plants need to live. Key words: habitat, change, need, shelter, evidence, clue.","The big idea is cause and need. Every change has a living thing behind it and a reason. A beaver dam makes a pond deep enough for the beaver's lodge, with the door under water, so it is safe from predators and can reach food in winter. Squirrels bury nuts to eat later, and the ones they forget can grow into new trees. Worms pull leaves into their tunnels to eat and leave curly casts on the lawn. Elephants dig in dry riverbeds to reach water.","Common mistakes: thinking only animals change places, because plants seem too soft to push; thinking a dam stops the stream so it dries up everywhere, when in fact the water backs up into a pond and then flows on over or through the dam; saying animals make changes for no reason, or to be naughty; and naming who made a change without looking at the clues.","In the lab your child piles four sticks across a stream and watches a pond fill behind the dam, drags four animals to the place each one changes, grows a tree for four years until its roots crack a path, sorts six changes by the need they meet, and uses clue pictures to say who made each change."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, go clue hunting. Look for a path lifted by tree roots, worm casts on the grass, a nest in a hedge or holes dug by squirrels. For each one, ask: who made this change, and what did they need? Food, water or a safe home?","keyIdea":"Plants and animals change the places where they live, to get what they need. A beaver piles sticks across a stream, so the water backs up into a pond where its home is safe. A rabbit digs a burrow, a bird builds a nest and worms make tunnels in the soil. Plants change places too: tree roots grow thicker and can crack a path. People change places as well, like digging a well for water.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Did a plant or an animal make this change? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A plant did it","An animal did it"],"items":[["Roots crack a path",0],["A dam blocks a stream",1],["Tunnels in the soil",1],["Roots split a rock",0],["A nest in a hedge",1],["Ivy covers a wall",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbeaver","caption":"Flagship lab: build a beaver dam and watch a pond fill, match animals to places, grow roots that crack a path, sort changes by need, and solve clues.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yhhprm7dZK":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is humus?","choices":["Rotted plant and animal remains","A kind of rock","Tiny grains of sand"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the dark, crumbly part of soil.","explain":"Humus forms when dead leaves, plants and animals rot. It holds water and feeds plants."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why can clay soil get waterlogged?","choices":["Its tiny bits leave tiny gaps for water","Its bits are the biggest","It has no rock in it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the size of the gaps between clay bits.","explain":"Clay bits are tiny and packed close, so water moves through very slowly and the soil stays wet."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which soil is best for most vegetables?","choices":["Loam","Pure sand","Pure clay"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which soil is a mix that drains but still holds some water?","explain":"Loam mixes sand, silt, clay and humus. It drains well but holds enough water and food for most plants."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How long can it take to make 1 cm of soil?","choices":["Up to 1,000 years","About a week","About a year"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rock breaks down very, very slowly.","explain":"The FAO says it can take up to 1,000 years to form 1 cm of soil, so soil lost to erosion is not quickly replaced."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What starts to break rock into the bits that make soil?","choices":["Rain, ice and plant roots","Worms eating the rock","People stamping on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of water freezing in a crack.","explain":"Rain wears rock away, water freezes and widens cracks, and roots push in. This breaking up is called weathering."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"100 ml of water is poured on clay soil. Only {b} ml drains through. How many ml did the clay hold?","answer":"100-b","params":{"b":[10,25]},"hint":"Take the water that drained away from 100 ml.","explain":"100 − {b} = {=100-b} ml stayed in the clay. Clay holds water because its gaps are tiny."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A jar of soil settles into layers: {s} cm of sand, {t} cm of silt and 1 cm of clay. How tall is the soil in the jar?","answer":"s+t+1","params":{"s":[3,6],"t":[2,4]},"hint":"Add the three layers together.","explain":"{s} + {t} + 1 = {=s+t+1} cm. Sand is at the bottom because its grains are the biggest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ella says, 'Soil is just crushed-up rock.' What is missing?","choices":["Humus, water, air and living things","Nothing, she is right","Only the worms"],"answer":0,"hint":"What else did you put in the jar besides rock bits?","explain":"Rock bits are only about half of soil. It also has humus, water, air and living things like worms and microbes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why is clay used to make pots?","choices":["Wet clay is sticky and can be shaped","It drains water fast","It floats in water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how clay feels when it is wet.","explain":"Wet clay is sticky and holds its shape, and it goes hard when it is baked, so it makes good pots and bricks."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Rocks and soil: what is soil made of?","choices":["Rock bits, humus, water, air and living things","Only crushed rock","Only rotted leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of everything you can find in a spoon of garden soil.","explain":"Soil is a mix. It has rock bits and humus, with water and air in the gaps, and living things like worms and microbes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You pour {a}0 ml of water on sandy soil. {b}0 ml drains through. How many ml stayed in the soil?","answer":"a*10-b*10","params":{"a":[10,12],"b":[6,8]},"hint":"Take the water that came out away from the water you poured in.","explain":"{a}0 − {b}0 = {=a*10-b*10} ml stayed in the soil. Sandy soil lets most water through."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A garden bed has {n} cm of soil. It took about 500 years to make each cm. About how many years did it take?","answer":"n*500","params":{"n":[2,5]},"hint":"Add 500 years once for each cm of soil.","explain":"{n} × 500 = {=n*500} years. Soil forms very slowly, so we look after it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You shake soil in a jar of water. What settles at the bottom first?","choices":["Sand, the biggest bits","Clay, the tiniest bits","Humus, the rotted leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which bits are biggest and heaviest?","explain":"Big, heavy sand grains sink first. Silt settles next and tiny clay bits settle last. Humus floats."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which soil lets water drain through fastest?","choices":["Sandy soil","Clay soil","They are all the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which soil has the biggest gaps between its bits?","explain":"Sand grains are big, so the gaps between them are big. Water rushes through sandy soil."}],"explain":{"kid":["Dig up a spoon of soil and look closely. You will see tiny bits of rock, dark crumbly bits and maybe a worm. Soil is a mix. It has bits of rock, humus, water, air and living things. Humus is what is left when dead leaves, plants and animals rot.","The rock bits come in sizes. Sand grains are the biggest. You can feel them. Silt is smaller. Clay bits are so tiny you cannot see one on its own. Shake soil in a jar of water. The big sand grains sink first. Clay settles last, on top. Humus floats.","Sandy soil has big gaps between its grains, so water rushes through. It dries out fast. Clay soil has tiny gaps, so it holds water. It feels sticky when wet and cracks when dry. Loam is a mix of sand, silt, clay and humus. Most plants grow well in it.","Soil is made very slowly. Rain, ice and plant roots crack rock into bits. Plants grow, die and rot into humus. Worms mix it all together. It can take up to 1,000 years to make just 1 cm of soil. That is why we look after it."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This is National Curriculum Year 3 Rocks: 'recognise that soils are made from rocks and organic matter', with the non-statutory suggestion to explore different kinds of soil. Key words: soil, humus, organic matter, sand, silt, clay, loam, drain, settle, weathering.","Facts used: USDA texture classes put sand at 0.05 to 2 mm, silt at 0.002 to 0.05 mm and clay under 0.002 mm. A typical loam is about 45% mineral, 5% organic matter, 25% water and 25% air by volume. The FAO (2015) says it can take up to 1,000 years to form 1 cm of soil, so soil is not quickly renewable. The 80, 50 and 15 ml in the lab's water test are a demonstration, in the order real drainage tests give.","Common mistakes: thinking soil is just crushed rock, or just 'dirt'; thinking the tiniest bits sink first in the shake test; thinking clay drains fastest because it feels smooth; thinking sand is best for all plants; and thinking soil forms in a few years. Some children also think worms eat rock. They do not, but they mix humus into the soil.","The lab has children fill a jar with soil's five parts (plastic and sugar are refused), shake it and name the layers bottom first, pour 100 ml through three soils and read the cylinders, match soils to jobs, and put the steps of soil-making in order."]},"familyTip":"Do a shake test together. Half-fill a clear jar with garden soil, top it up with water, add the lid and shake hard. Leave it on a windowsill and look after a minute, an hour and a day. Ask which layer settled first and why, and what is floating on top.","keyIdea":"Soil is not just crushed rock. It is a mix of tiny bits of rock, humus (dark, rotted plant and animal remains), water, air and living things such as worms and microbes. In a typical garden loam, about 45% is rock bits, 25% water, 25% air and 5% humus. The rock bits come in sizes: sand grains are the biggest, silt is smaller and clay bits are too tiny to see. Shake soil in a jar of water and the biggest bits sink first: sand at the bottom, then silt, then clay, with humus floating. Sandy soil has big gaps, so water drains through fast and it dries out. Clay soil has tiny gaps, so it holds water and can get waterlogged. Loam is a mix that suits most plants. Soil forms very slowly: rain, ice and roots break rock, and plants rot into humus. It can take up to 1,000 years to make 1 cm of soil.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sandy soil or clay soil? Drag each fact into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Sandy soil","Clay soil"],"items":[["Water drains through fast",0],["Holds water and gets sticky",1],["Big grains you can feel",0],["Tiny bits, too small to see",1],["Dries out quickly in sun",0],["Good for making pots",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsoil","caption":"Flagship lab: fill a jar with the parts of soil, shake it and name the layers, pour water through three soils, match soils to jobs and order how soil is made.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_7B3vNwvUEE":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where does a camel live?","choices":["In the hot desert","In the Arctic","In the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"A camel can go for days without a drink. Where is it dry?","explain":"A camel lives in the hot, dry desert. It can go a long time without water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Do penguins and polar bears live together?","choices":["No, they live far apart","Yes, in the Arctic","Yes, in the desert"],"answer":0,"hint":"Polar bears live in the far north. Where do wild penguins live?","explain":"Polar bears live in the Arctic in the north. Wild penguins live in the south, many near Antarctica."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where do most people in the Arctic live?","choices":["In houses in towns and villages","Nobody lives there","Only in igloos"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the town in the Arctic in the lab.","explain":"Most people in the Arctic live in houses, in towns and villages, and go to school and work."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Clue: there is lots of sand. Which places could it be?","choices":["A hot desert or the seaside","Only the Arctic","Only the rainforest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where have you seen sand? Is there more than one place?","explain":"Sand fits a hot desert and the seaside. You need another clue to know which."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"In one week it rained on {a} days in the rainforest and 0 days in the desert. How many more rainy days did the rainforest have?","answer":"a","hint":"No rain in the desert means 0 rainy days. What is {a} take away 0?","explain":"{a} take away 0 is {a}, so the rainforest had {a} more rainy days. It is much wetter.","params":{"a":[4,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You see {n} animals at the seaside. {k} are crabs. The rest are seagulls. How many seagulls?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} and take away the {k} crabs.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} seagulls.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the same in your town and a town in the Arctic?","choices":["People live in houses","There is snow all year","Polar bears walk past"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of things that are in both pictures.","explain":"People, families and houses are in both towns. The snow and ice are different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why do tall trees grow in a rainforest?","choices":["It is warm and it rains a lot","It is cold and icy","It never rains"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do plants need lots of to grow big?","explain":"Plants need water and warmth. A rainforest has both, so tall trees grow there."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which place is cold and icy?","choices":["The Arctic","The hot desert","The rainforest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do polar bears live?","explain":"The Arctic is cold, with ice and snow. Polar bears live there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"It rained on {a} days in the rainforest. It rained on {b} more days the next week. How many rainy days is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more days.","explain":"{a} rainy days and {b} more make {=a+b} rainy days. A rainforest is very wet.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {n} camels in the hot desert. {k} walk away. How many camels are left?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} and take away {k}.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} camels in the desert.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A rainforest and a hot desert are both hot. What is different?","choices":["The rainforest is wet, the desert is dry","The desert is wet, the rainforest is dry","Nothing is different"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has lots of rain and tall trees?","explain":"Both are hot, but it rains most days in a rainforest and hardly ever in a hot desert."}],"explain":{"kid":["There are lots of places in the world. Some are not like your home. Some are cold, with ice and snow. Some are hot, with strong sun.","The Arctic is cold. It has ice and snow. Polar bears live there. A hot desert is hot and dry. It hardly ever rains. Camels live there.","A rainforest is hot too. But it is wet. It rains most days, so tall trees grow. Parrots and monkeys live in the trees.","People live in lots of places, too. A child in the Arctic goes to school and lives in a house, like you. Some things are the same. Some things are different."],"adult":["Development Matters is the guidance for England's Early Years Foundation Stage. It asks children to talk about places that are different from home, using what they have seen and photos. By the end of Reception, the Early Learning Goals expect them to know some ways that contrasting environments are alike and different. This lesson builds that habit with four places: the Arctic, a hot desert, a rainforest and the seaside.","The key skill is to compare. Look at two places side by side. Say what is the same and what is different. Give your child the words: environment, weather, hot, cold, wet, dry, ice, sand, forest, coast, same and different. Start from what your child knows, like your garden, park or a beach trip. Then use photos, picture books, a map or a globe.","Watch for some common mistakes. Many children think penguins and polar bears live together. In fact, polar bears live in the Arctic in the north, and wild penguins live in the south. Some think a rainforest is dry because it is hot. Some think nobody lives in the Arctic, or that everyone there lives in an igloo. Be careful with ideas like these about people: most Arctic families live in houses and go to school and the shops, just as yours does.","In the lab your child sorts places into hot and cold and watches a week of rain in a rainforest and a hot desert. Next they take four animals home. Then they decide what is the same and what is different in Sam's town and a town in the Arctic. Last, they solve two mystery places from clues."]},"familyTip":"Look at a photo of a faraway place together, from a book or a postcard. Ask: 'Is it hot or cold? Wet or dry? What is the same as our home? What is different?'","keyIdea":"The world has many kinds of places, or environments. The Arctic and Antarctica are cold and icy. A hot desert is hot and dry. A rainforest is hot and wet. The seaside is where the land meets the sea. Plants, animals and the way people live suit each place. We can compare a place with our own home: some things are the same, like people, houses and families, and some are different, like the weather and the animals.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it from a cold place or a hot place? Drag each one into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Cold place","Hot place"],"items":[["Polar bear",0],["Ice and snow",0],["Penguin",0],["Icebergs",0],["Camel",1],["Sand dunes",1],["Parrot in tall trees",1],["Strong sun",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplaces","caption":"Flagship lab: sort places into hot and cold, watch a week of rain, take animals home, spot what is the same and different, and solve mystery places from clues.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KsKLVW_ssY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is water with land all round it?","choices":["a lake","the ocean","a river"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture water that is closed in by land on every side.","explain":"A lake has land all round it. The ocean is huge and not closed in, and a river is a long line of moving water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which water is salty?","choices":["the ocean","a river","a pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the water at the beach.","explain":"Ocean water is salty. Rivers, ponds and most lakes are fresh water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which one is wide, flat, low land?","choices":["a plain","a mountain","a hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for land that is low and does not go up and down.","explain":"A plain is flat and low. Mountains and hills are high land that rises up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where does a river start?","choices":["on high land","in the sea","at the bottom of a lake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rivers flow downhill. So where must the start be?","explain":"A river starts on high land, from rain or melting snow, and flows down to a lake or the sea."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"On a map, what does blue show?","choices":["water","mountains","snow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the colour of the sea on a map.","explain":"Blue shows water: the ocean, lakes, ponds and rivers. Mountains are often brown and snow is white."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A river is {a} km long. It flows {b} km down through the mountains, then the rest of the way across a flat plain to the sea. How many km does it flow across the plain?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the mountain part away from the whole length of the river.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}, so the river flows {=a-b} km across the plain. It starts high in the mountains and ends low at the sea.","params":{"a":[8,12],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A mountain is {=a*100} metres high. The hill next to it is {=b*100} metres high. How many metres higher is the mountain?","answer":"(a-b)*100","hint":"Mountains are higher than hills. Take the hill's height away from the mountain's height.","explain":"{=a*100} − {=b*100} = {=(a-b)*100}, so the mountain is {=(a-b)*100} metres higher. A mountain is higher and steeper than a hill.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is a valley a kind of land or a kind of water?","choices":["land","water","both at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"A valley can have a river in it, but what is the valley itself?","explain":"A valley is low land between hills or mountains. A river often runs along it, but the valley is the land."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is low land between hills or mountains?","choices":["a valley","a plain","a lake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of land with high sides on both left and right.","explain":"A valley is low land with hills or mountains on each side. A plain is flat land with no high sides, and a lake is water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A river starts on a mountain {=a*100} metres high. It flows all the way down to the sea, which is at 0 metres. How many metres does it go down?","answer":"a*100","hint":"The sea is the lowest place, at 0 metres. Rivers always flow downhill to it.","explain":"The river goes from {=a*100} metres down to 0 metres, so it drops {=a*100} metres. Water flows downhill the whole way.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"On a map, a river crosses {a} squares of mountains and then {b} squares of flat plain before it reaches the sea. How many squares long is the river?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the mountain squares, then count on the plain squares.","explain":"{a} mountain squares and {b} plain squares make {=a+b} squares. The river starts high in the mountains and ends low at the sea.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Rain falls on top of a hill. Where does the water go?","choices":["It runs down to lower land","It stays on top","It flows up to the next hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pour water on a slope. Which way does it always run?","explain":"Water always runs downhill. It ends up in a stream, a river, a lake or the sea, which sit in the low places."}],"explain":{"kid":["Land is not flat everywhere. A mountain is very high land with a steep top. A hill is high too, but lower and rounder. A valley is low land between hills or mountains. A plain is wide, flat, low land.","Water has shapes too. The ocean is huge and salty. A lake is water with land all round it. A pond is a small lake. A river is a long line of water that flows.","Water always runs downhill. Rain on a mountain runs down the sides. Small streams join to make a river. The river flows down the valley to a lake or the sea.","A map shows land and water from above. Blue is water. Green is low land. Brown is high land. If you slice a map and look from the side, you see the hills and valleys."],"adult":["The US Grade 2 standard 2-ESS2-2 asks children to 'develop a model to represent the shapes and kinds of land and bodies of water in an area'. In England, Year 1 and Year 2 geography asks pupils to use words for physical features. These include hill, mountain, valley, sea, ocean, river and lake.","The big idea is height. Each landform is high or low compared with the land around it. Water always flows downhill, so it collects in the lowest places. About 71% of Earth's surface is water, and nearly all of it is salty ocean. Rivers and most lakes hold fresh water.","Common mistakes: thinking a valley is a hole or a lake, thinking rivers can flow uphill or start at the sea, and calling the ocean a big lake. Some children think all water is salty. On a map, children often read blue only as the sea, so point out that rivers and lakes are blue too.","In the lab your child drags the land to build a mountain, a valley and a plain. They make rain that runs downhill into the ocean or pools as a lake. Then they name features on a map, start rivers on high ground to see where they end, and slice the map to see the land from the side."]},"familyTip":"On a walk or a car trip, look for high and low land: is that a hill or a mountain, and where would rain on it run? Find a stream or a drain after rain and follow the water downhill together. At home, pour a cup of water onto a pile of sand and watch where it goes.","keyIdea":"Land has shapes: mountains and hills are high, valleys are low land between them, and plains are wide and flat. Water has shapes too: oceans are huge and salty, lakes and ponds have land all round them, and rivers are long lines of fresh water. Water always flows downhill, so rivers run from high land to a lake or the sea, and a map shows all of these from above.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a shape of land or a kind of water? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Land","Water"],"items":[["mountain",0],["valley",0],["plain",0],["hill",0],["ocean",1],["lake",1],["river",1],["pond",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagland","caption":"Flagship lab: shape a mountain, valley and plain, make rain run downhill, name a map, start rivers on high land and slice the map.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mwirOvigWD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these can become a fossil?","choices":["a leaf that was buried in mud","a plastic spoon","a glass marble"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossils come from things that once lived.","explain":"A leaf is from a living plant. Plastic and glass are not from living things."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sediment layers are mostly…","choices":["mud and sand that settle","melted plastic","fresh paint"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of what rivers and seas drop on the bottom.","explain":"Sediment is natural mud, sand and silt that settle in water or wind."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A footprint in rock can be a fossil because…","choices":["an animal once stood there and the print was buried","someone drew it yesterday with chalk","it is made of shiny metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"The print must come from a real living animal long ago.","explain":"Trace fossils like footprints form when an animal presses into soft ground that later turns to rock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is a plastic dinosaur toy not a fossil?","choices":["it was never a living thing trapped in rock","it is the wrong colour","fossils cannot look like dinosaurs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: did this toy once live, die and get buried for millions of years?","explain":"Toys are made by people. Fossils form from real living things trapped in rock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"After burial, what piles on top over time?","choices":["more layers of mud and sand","only fresh air","bubble wrap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Year after year, more sediment settles.","explain":"More sediment layers build up and squash what is underneath."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"When you dig a fossil carefully, you are trying to…","choices":["keep the shape inside the rock whole","melt the rock with water","eat the rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossils can crack if you hit them hard.","explain":"Careful digging protects the fragile fossil shape preserved in the rock."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A tray shows {n} fossil shells and you add 3 more. How many shells now?","answer":"n+3","hint":"Start with {n} and add three.","explain":"{n} + 3 = {=n+3} fossil shells.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A model uses {n} layers of sand. Each layer is 2 cm thick. How many cm tall is the stack?","answer":"n*2","hint":"Multiply the number of layers by 2 cm.","explain":"{n} layers × 2 cm = {=n*2} cm.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a fossil?","choices":["a shape of a living thing trapped in rock","any old stone on the path","a plastic model of a dinosaur"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about something that once lived, then got trapped for a very long time.","explain":"A fossil is the shape left by a living thing that was buried and turned into rock over a very long time."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What must happen first for a fossil to form?","choices":["the living thing is buried in mud or sand","someone paints the bones","the bones are left in bright sun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Burial protects the shape so it is not eaten or washed away.","explain":"Burial under mud or sand is the first step. Without it, the body usually rots or is scattered."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How long does soft mud usually take to become hard fossil rock?","choices":["millions of years","about one week","one sunny afternoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossil rock forms on a deep-time scale, not a school-week scale.","explain":"Turning sediment into rock takes millions of years of pressure and time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A fossil dig finds {n} shell fossils and 2 bone fossils. How many fossils in total?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Add the shell fossils to the bone fossils.","explain":"{n} shells plus 2 bones makes {=n+2} fossils.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Four sediment layers pile up each year for {n} years in a model. How many layers is that?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Four layers every year, for that many years.","explain":"4 layers × {n} years = {=4*n} layers in the model.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A fossil is a shape in rock. It came from a plant or animal that lived a very long time ago.","First the plant or animal dies and gets covered by soft mud or sand. More mud piles on, layer after layer.","Over millions of years the mud is squashed and turns into hard rock. The shape stays trapped inside.","When we dig the rock, we can find that shape: a shell, a bone, a leaf mark or a footprint. That shape is the fossil."],"adult":["Year 3 science in England asks children to describe in simple terms how fossils are formed when things that have lived are trapped within rock (NC Y3 Rocks). Keep the story short: die and bury, sediment layers, deep time, rock, then the preserved shape.","Words to use: fossil, sediment, mud, sand, layers, rock, millions of years, shell, bone, imprint, footprint. A common mistake is thinking any old stone is a fossil, or that plastic toys and metal coins count. Fossils come from living things. Another slip is thinking fossils form in days or weeks; the rock step takes millions of years.","In the lab your child buries a shell, piles sediment layers, waits for rock, digs the fossil out, then sorts finds into fossil or not."]},"familyTip":"Look at a fossil picture book or a museum photo together. Ask 'What living thing left this shape?' and 'What covered it so it did not rot away?' On a beach, talk about shells sinking into wet sand as a tiny first step of the same idea.","keyIdea":"A fossil is the shape of something that once lived, trapped inside rock. First the plant or animal dies and gets buried in mud or sand. More layers pile on. Over millions of years that soft mud hardens into rock, and the shape stays inside. Digging later can reveal a shell, bone, leaf imprint or footprint fossil.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fossil or not? Drag each find, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fossil","Not a fossil"],"items":[["Shell shape in rock",0],["Plastic toy dinosaur",1],["Bone in rock",0],["Shiny new coin",1],["Leaf imprint in rock",0],["Footprint in rock",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfoss","caption":"Flagship lab: bury a shell, pile sediment layers, wait millions of years for rock, dig out the fossil, and sort fossil vs not.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nRF_VRntrW":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where is most of Earth's fresh water?","choices":["frozen as ice","in rivers and lakes","in the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most fresh water is in glaciers and ice sheets.","explain":"About 69 of every 100 parts of fresh water are ice. Rivers and lakes are a tiny sliver, and the sea is salty."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is water under the ground like?","choices":["water in tiny gaps in rock and soil","a big underground lake","there is none"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the well in the lab. What did the pipe reach?","explain":"Groundwater fills the tiny gaps between bits of rock and soil, like water in a sponge. A well pumps it up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Can people drink sea water?","choices":["No, it is too salty","Yes, it is just water","Yes, if it is cold"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what makes sea water different from river water.","explain":"Sea water is salty, so it is not safe to drink. Cooling it does not take the salt out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"From the 100-cup model, you put all 3 fresh cups on a table, then add {n} salty ocean cups. How many cups are on the table?","answer":"3+n","hint":"Start with the 3 fresh cups, then count on the salty cups you added.","explain":"3 + {n} = {=3+n} cups. Only 3 of them are fresh; the other {n} are salty sea water.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A class finds water in {n} lakes, {k} rivers and 1 ocean. How many of these places hold fresh water?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Lakes and rivers are fresh water. The ocean is salty.","explain":"{n} lakes + {k} rivers = {=n+k} places with fresh water. The ocean is salty, so it is not counted.","params":{"n":[1,4],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An iceberg floats in the salty sea. What is it made of?","choices":["frozen fresh water","frozen salty water","white rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"An iceberg breaks off the end of a glacier.","explain":"An iceberg broke off a glacier, which formed from snow, so it is frozen fresh water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which one is solid water?","choices":["snow","rain","a pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Solid water keeps its shape. Liquid water flows.","explain":"Snow is frozen water, so it is solid. Rain and pond water are liquid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Out of 100 cups of Earth's water, about how many are salty?","choices":["97","50","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most of Earth is covered by salty oceans.","explain":"About 97 of every 100 cups are salty. Only about 3 cups are fresh."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where is most of the water on Earth?","choices":["in the oceans","in rivers","in clouds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the 100 cups. Which jar filled almost all of them?","explain":"The oceans hold about 97 of every 100 cups of Earth's water. Rivers and clouds hold only a little."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which of these is fresh water?","choices":["a lake","the sea","the ocean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fresh water has very little salt. Seas and oceans are salty.","explain":"A lake is fresh water. The sea and the ocean are salty water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You fill {n} cups with sea water and {k} cups with rain water. How many cups hold fresh water?","answer":"k","hint":"Sea water is salty. Rain water is fresh. Count only the fresh cups.","explain":"Only the rain water is fresh, so {k} cups hold fresh water. The {n} cups of sea water are salty.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,5]},"where":"n!=k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Out of every 100 cups of Earth's water, 2 cups are ice. How many cups are ice out of {=100*n} cups?","answer":"2*n","hint":"There are {n} lots of 100 cups. Each 100 has 2 cups of ice.","explain":"{n} lots of 2 cups is {=2*n} cups of ice out of {=100*n} cups.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Is a glacier solid or liquid water?","choices":["solid","liquid","it is not water"],"answer":0,"hint":"A glacier is a huge sheet of ice that moves very slowly.","explain":"A glacier is ice, so it is solid water. It is fresh water, made from snow."}],"explain":{"kid":["Water is in lots of places. It is in the sea and the ocean. It is in rivers, lakes and ponds. It is in clouds, and it is even under the ground.","Sea water is salty. You must not drink it. Rivers, lakes and rain are fresh water, with very little salt.","Water can be a liquid that flows, like a river. It can also be solid, like ice and snow. A glacier is a huge river of ice. Its ice is fresh water.","Pretend all the water on Earth fills 100 cups. 97 cups are salty sea water. Only 3 cups are fresh. Most of those are ice. Lakes and rivers are just a few drops."],"adult":["This topic matches NGSS 2-ESS2-3: obtain information to identify where water is found on Earth and that it can be solid or liquid. Useful words: ocean, sea, salt water, fresh water, river, lake, pond, glacier, ice sheet, iceberg, groundwater, solid, liquid.","The figures come from the USGS summary of Earth's water (after Gleick, 1996). Oceans and seas hold about 96.5% of all water; with salty groundwater and salt lakes, about 97.5% is salty. About 2.5% is fresh. Of the fresh water, about 69% is frozen in ice caps and glaciers, about 30% is groundwater, and only about 1% is in lakes, rivers, soil, swamps and the air. The 100-cup model rounds these to 97 salty, 2 ice, 1 underground and a few drops for lakes and rivers.","Common mistakes: thinking most fresh water is in rivers and lakes, because those are the fresh water children see; thinking groundwater is an underground lake or river, when it mostly fills the small gaps between grains of soil and cracks in rock; thinking ice and snow are not water; and thinking an iceberg is salty because it floats in the sea, when it broke off a glacier made from snow.","In the lab your child sorts water places into salty, fresh and frozen, pours Earth's water into 100 cups, puts ice, groundwater and lakes on the right share of the fresh water, digs a well down to the wet rock and pumps it up, then finds every place with fresh water on a map."]},"familyTip":"Pour 100 small spoonfuls of water into a big bowl. Take out 3 spoonfuls for 'fresh water'. Put 2 of them in the freezer for ice. Then ask: 'How much is left for every lake and river?' (just a drop from the last spoon).","keyIdea":"Water is found in oceans and seas, in rivers, lakes and ponds, frozen in glaciers, ice sheets and snow, under the ground and in clouds. Almost all of it, about 97 of every 100 cups, is salty ocean water. Only about 3 cups are fresh, and most of that fresh water is frozen ice. Fresh water under the ground fills tiny gaps in rock and soil. Lakes and rivers hold only a few drops of the 100 cups. Water can be liquid, like a river, or solid, like ice.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Solid or liquid? Drag each kind of water to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Solid water","Liquid water"],"items":[["an ice cube",0],["rain",1],["a puddle",1],["an icicle",0],["a snowflake",0],["a river",1],["hailstones",0],["the sea",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwater","caption":"Flagship lab: sort salty, fresh and frozen water, pour 100 cups, split the fresh water, dig a well and find fresh water on a map.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_m6UaSmrQVG":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is erosion?","choices":["Soil, sand or rock carried away by water or wind","A hill growing taller","Seeds growing in soil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the rain did to the bare hill.","explain":"Erosion is the land being carried away, mostly by moving water and wind."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do plant roots help stop erosion?","choices":["They grip the soil and hold it together","They make the soil wet","They push the soil downhill"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture the roots under a clump of grass.","explain":"Roots spread through the soil like a net and hold it in place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What do terraces (flat steps on a hill) do?","choices":["Slow the water running down","Make the rain heavier","Move the hill away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Water runs fast down a long slope. What do flat steps change?","explain":"Flat steps break up the slope, so water slows down and carries less soil."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sand blows off a dune onto a road. What is the best fix?","choices":["Plant marram grass and put up a sand fence","Sweep the road every morning","Flatten the dune"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fix the place where the sand starts to move.","explain":"Grass and fences on the dune trap the sand before it blows. Sweeping only clears it after."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A wall is built at the bottom of a muddy slope. What does it do?","choices":["Catches soil that slides down","Stops rain falling on the slope","Makes the slope grow"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, did the wall stop the soil moving on the slope itself?","explain":"The wall holds soil back at the bottom, but the slope above can still wear away. Plants help there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which would make erosion worse?","choices":["Cutting down the trees on a river bank","Planting grass on a slope","Putting a hedge on the windy side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one takes away roots?","explain":"Without roots to hold it, a river bank falls in and washes away faster."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A row of trees is {h} m tall. Calm air reaches about 10 times its height. The field is {f} m long. How many metres of field are NOT sheltered?","answer":"f-h*10","params":{"h":[3,5],"f":[60,90]},"hint":"Work out the sheltered length (height × 10), then take it from the field's length.","explain":"{h} × 10 = {=h*10} m sheltered, so {f} − {=h*10} = {=f-h*10} m is not. A taller row of trees or a second row would help."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Four trays lose 80 g, {a} g, {b} g and 10 g of soil. What is the total soil lost?","answer":"90+a+b","params":{"a":[20,40],"b":[15,30]},"hint":"Add all four amounts together.","explain":"80 + {a} + {b} + 10 = {=90+a+b} g in total."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why should every tray get the same rain in a test?","choices":["So only the fix is different","So the test is quicker","So the soil stays dry"],"answer":0,"hint":"What are you trying to find out?","explain":"If only the fix changes, any difference in soil lost must come from the fix. That is a fair test."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which hill loses the least soil in heavy rain?","choices":["A hill covered in grass","A bare hill","A freshly dug hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, which tray had the least soil in its catch tray?","explain":"Grass leaves soften the rain and roots grip the soil, so a grassy hill loses far less soil than bare ground."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A bare tray loses {b} g of soil. A grassy tray loses {g} g in the same rain. How many more grams does the bare tray lose?","answer":"b-g","params":{"b":[60,95],"g":[5,15]},"hint":"Take the grassy tray's soil away from the bare tray's soil.","explain":"{b} − {g} = {=b-g} g more soil lost from bare ground. That is the difference the grass makes."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Wind blows from the west across a field. Where should the farmer plant a windbreak?","choices":["On the west side","On the east side","In the middle of the field"],"answer":0,"hint":"The windbreak must meet the wind before the field does.","explain":"The wind comes from the west, so the trees go on the west side. The calm air is behind them, over the field."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A hedge is {h} m tall. The air behind it is calm for about 10 times its height. How many metres of field does it shelter?","answer":"h*10","params":{"h":[2,9]},"hint":"Multiply the height by 10.","explain":"{h} × 10 = {=h*10} m of sheltered field behind the hedge."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam tests two fixes. She pours 1 cup of water on one tray and 3 cups on the other. What is wrong?","choices":["The rain is not the same, so it is not fair","Nothing, it is a fair test","She should use no water"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a fair test, how many things should change?","explain":"Only the fix should change. Both trays need the same amount of water, or the results cannot be compared."}],"explain":{"kid":["Erosion is when soil, sand or rock is carried away. Rain running down a hill, a river, waves and wind can all carry the ground away, bit by bit.","Plants are one of the best fixes. Their leaves catch the rain so the drops hit softly. Their roots grip the soil like a net. A bare hill loses much more soil than a grassy one.","Other fixes slow the water down. Flat steps cut into a hill, called terraces, stop the water racing down. A wall at the bottom of a slope catches soil that slides down.","Wind blows dry soil and sand away. A row of trees or a hedge is a windbreak. Put it on the side the wind comes from, and the air behind it is calm. To find the best fix, test each one fairly with the same rain or wind."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This supports NGSS 2-ESS2-1 (compare multiple solutions designed to slow or prevent wind or water from changing the shape of the land) and the National Curriculum in England (working scientifically: fair tests and comparing results; geography: physical processes). Facts follow USDA NRCS conservation practice standards (windbreaks, terraces, critical area planting), National Geographic and the British Geological Survey.","The lab has five tasks. Children sort water and wind erosion, rain on four model hills (bare, grass, a wall at the foot, terraces) with the same rain each time and read the soil caught, drag a hedge until it shelters a whole field, choose fixes for four real problems, and judge three classmates. The gram figures are for the model; their order matches field studies, where dense grass can cut soil loss to a few per cent of bare ground.","Common mistakes. Children often think plants help because they 'drink the rain', put a windbreak on the downwind side, think a wall stops erosion everywhere, or treat the symptom (sweeping sand off a road) instead of the cause. Be careful about fair tests: many children give the fix they like an easier test. Stress that only one thing should change."]},"familyTip":"Fill two trays or old baking tins with damp soil and tilt them the same way. Press a clump of grass or some moss into one. Pour the same cup of water slowly over each and catch what runs off in two jars. Which jar is muddier? Then look for erosion fixes on a walk: hedges, grassy banks, steps on a hillside path, or rocks along a river.","keyIdea":"Erosion is when water or wind carries soil, sand or rock away. People slow it down with fixes that hold the ground or slow the water and wind. Plants are a strong fix: their leaves soften the rain and their roots grip the soil, so a grassy hill loses far less soil than a bare one. Flat steps (terraces) slow water running down a hill, and a wall at the bottom catches soil that slides down. A row of trees or a hedge on the windy side of a field is a windbreak: the air behind it is calm. To find the best fix, test each one fairly, with the same rain or wind.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it slow erosion or make it worse? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Slows erosion","Makes it worse"],"items":[["Planting grass on a slope",0],["Pulling up plants",1],["A hedge on the windy side",0],["Ploughing dry soil on a windy day",1],["Cutting steps into a hill",0],["Flattening a sand dune",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagerode","caption":"Flagship lab: sort water from wind erosion, rain on four model hills, drag a hedge to shelter a field and choose fixes for real problems.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
